														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2754591">
									

<p>I'm largely having the same problems as Shaggy. The description of the violent behaviors weren't particularly clear or imaginable. It seems like trying to write up the skit (which I've seen) in clinical tone with perfect detail just probably isn't going to make sense. I'd try to simplify. Don't make everything hard and fast rules (it only knows period weaponry, it can change weapons but thinks they are harmful but they aren't). It isn't interesting to read, and it doesn't add to th SCP.</p>
<p>The dumb-but-show-accurate tantrums also really don't fit with his tone in the conversation on page 2. I wouldn't change the conversation, because that's definitely the best part.</p>
<p>Other than that&#8230; You're being too clever by half. The altering of the -A document immediately reminded me of the crown cognitihazard, which isn't a comparison you want to invite: you'll lose. You also never mentioned within the -A document that it alters it's own document format, so there's this vague meta level of confusion on top of the rest of the vagueness going on.<br />
- So, you might as well make the picture match the actor, given that our expectations are 100% altered. That, or ditch the format changes, or push the format changes even further towards being Monty Python-esque, rather than just being slightly modified Foundation format. I guess the main way to do that would be, I donno, pictures, or &quot;Sir 2476 the Safe&quot; instated of the split lines.</p>
<p>No vote for now. I love the characterisation, but this needs cleanup.</p>

